 "_about": "Clothes pieces sewn onto the rigged characters by tools/tailor.py (run it after editing this file; it writes assets/models/outfits/wear/<piece>_<base>_<body>.gltf). An outfit in data/characters.json lists the pieces it wears. Each piece is a list of parts; a part has a material 'kind' (cloth, leather, metal, thatch ...: the fantasy props' materials), a 'color' (r, g, b as seen on screen) and EITHER a 'shell' OR a 'sheet'. shell = faces of the clothes copied and pushed out by 'grow' metres: 'bones' = the faces that follow these bones (left and right together; fingers by their plain names), 'band' = only between two heights, each [bone, metres above it], 'along' = only this stretch of a limb bone (0 = its joint, 1 = the next joint), 'no_arms' = leave out the arms, 'front' = 1 only what faces forward, -1 backward. sheet = a new panel round the body: 'arc' = from and to bearing in degrees (0 = straight ahead, 90 = the body's left, -180..180 = all round), 'top' and 'bottom' heights as [bone, metres above it], 'fit' = how close it sits at the top, 'loose' = how far out further down, 'flare' = how much each row widens, 'hem' = a dagged hem (every other column of the last row that much higher), 'taper' = how much a row may come back in under a wider one (0 = cloth hangs straight down from the widest place), 'stiff' = how much of the lower rows follows 'stiff_bone' (default pelvis) instead of the legs. 'bases' = make it only for these base outfits (default both). Bones: Head, neck_01, spine_03 (chest), spine_02, spine_01 (waist), pelvis (hips), thigh, calf (knee), foot (ankle), clavicle, upperarm, lowerarm, hand.",
